  "question": {
    "text": "A plot of the number of neutrons <math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>N</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math> against the number of protons <math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>P</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math> of&nbsp;the stable nuclei exhibits upwards deviation from linearity for atomic number, <math> <mrow> <mtext>Z</mtext><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>20</mn> </mrow> </math>. For an unstable nucleus having <math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mtext>N</mtext> <mtext>P</mtext> </mfrac> </mrow> </math> ratio less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>, the possible mode(s) of decay is (are):"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#946;</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup></math> decay ( <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#946;</mi></math> emission)",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Orbital of K-electron capture",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "Neutron emission",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#946;</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> decay (positron emission)",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B",
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "As <math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> ratio is less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>,&nbsp;for possible decay mode,&nbsp;<math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> ratio should increase. The possible modes are <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#945;</mi></math>-decay, K-capture and <math><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#946;</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>-decay. Hence, the correct options are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>4</mn></mfenced></math>&nbsp;(In <math><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#946;</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup></math>-decay or neutron decay, <math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> ratio will decrease further).<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mfrac></math> should increase. So,<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></mmultiscripts><mo>&#10230;</mo><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn></mmultiscripts></math> [K-electron Capture]<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></mmultiscripts><mo>&#10230;</mo><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn></mmultiscripts><mo>+</mo><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#946;</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></mmultiscripts></math> [<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#946;</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>-decay]<br />&nbsp;"
  }
}